  "content": {
    "sequence": [
      {
        "step": 1,
        "text": "A request creates a favour. A schedule creates a default. Giving your partner a named shift is a status upgrade, not charity.",
        "type": "hook"
      },
      {
        "step": 2,
        "text": "Asking directly for help can feel like an accusation. The partner's one-off gesture resets the expectation: he helped once, so the default remains she owns all of it.",
        "type": "problem"
      },
      {
        "step": 3,
        "text": "Reframes the partner from 'helper' to 'co-manager' — a status upgrade instead of a request for charity. A written roster removes the emotional labour of asking: the schedule asks, not her.",
        "type": "insight"
      },
      {
        "step": 4,
        "text": "Nights with zero protected block become a protected block of 3+ hours on most nights. 'Whose turn' arguments drop from several times a week to once a week or less. The partner initiates his shift without being reminded.",
        "type": "cta"
      }
    ]
  },
